artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $d\ge 2$ be an integer. The Erdős–Rényi $d$-uniform random hypergraph $G_d(N,1/2)$ has vertex set $[N]=\{1,\dots,N\}$ and each of the $\binom{N}{d}$ possible $d$-element hyperedges is included independently with probability $1/2$. The planted model $G_d(N,1/2,\kappa)$ is obtained by first sampling $G_d(N,1/2)$, then choosing a uniformly random subset $K\subseteq[N]$ with $|K|=\kappa$ and adding every $d$-element subset of $K$ as a hyperedge (so $K$ becomes a hypergraphic clique).

The hypergraphic planted clique (HPC) detection problem asks, given a single sample, to distinguish
$$H_0:\ G\sim G_d(N,1/2)\quad\text{versus}\quad H_1:\ G\sim G_d(N,1/2,\kappa).$$
A (possibly randomized) test $\psi:\{\text{hypergraphs on }[N]\}\to\{0,1\}$ is said to solve detection asymptotically if
$$\mathbb{P}_{H_0}[\psi=1]+\mathbb{P}_{H_1}[\psi=0]\longrightarrow 0\quad\text{as }N\to\infty.$$
It is conjectured that, for any fixed $d\ge 3$ and any $\kappa=\kappa(N)$ with $\kappa\le N^{1/2-\varepsilon}$ for some $\varepsilon>0$, no polynomial-time test solves HPC detection.

Problem. Provide rigorous evidence for this conjectured computational hardness within additional restricted models of computation beyond Metropolis-type algorithms and low-degree polynomial tests. Specifically, prove unconditional lower bounds (matching the threshold $\kappa\asymp\sqrt{N}$) for the HPC detection problem under at least one of the following algorithmic frameworks:
(a) statistical query algorithms (with an appropriate query and tolerance model for hypergraph distributions);
(b) the sum-of-squares hierarchy, by exhibiting pseudo-expectations of degree growing with $N$ that certify indistinguishability when $\kappa=o(\sqrt{N})$;
(c) belief propagation on the associated factor graph;
(d) approximate message passing algorithms;
or any other natural restricted computational model.
